2. Ownership and reservation of rights

Subject to third-party rights, Mindclade, LLC. retains all right, title, and
interest in the Materials, including copyright, patent, trade-secret,
trademark, and other intellectual-property rights. All rights not expressly
granted in a controlling written agreement are reserved.

3. Confidentiality and safeguards

Where a duty of confidentiality applies, use the Materials only for the
authorized Mindclade purpose; restrict access to authorized people with a need
to know; apply reasonable administrative, technical, and physical safeguards;
do not disclose them without prior written authorization; and promptly report
suspected loss, misuse, or unauthorized access through an approved private
channel. Return or securely destroy Materials when authorization ends, except
where law or a controlling agreement requires retention.

4. Public and third-party materials

Information does not become confidential merely because this notice appears on
a copy. Material Mindclade has lawfully made public is not subject to the
confidentiality obligations above. Third-party materials remain governed by
their own licenses, notices, acceptable-use terms, and restrictions.

5. Trademarks and publicity

No right to use Mindclade names, logos, marks, trade dress, publicity, or
endorsement is granted. Authorized internal use must follow current brand rules
and the controlling agreement.

6. Protected disclosures

Nothing prohibits a disclosure protected by applicable law, including a
confidential report of suspected legal violations to a government authority or
legal counsel. Under 18 U.S.C. § 1833(b), an individual is not liable under
federal or state trade-secret law for a qualifying confidential disclosure to
a government official or attorney made solely to report or investigate a
suspected violation of law, or for a disclosure in a document filed under seal.

7. Termination and remedies

Authorization ends when the controlling agreement ends or access is lawfully
revoked. Ownership, confidentiality, third-party rights, protected disclosure,
remedy, and disclaimer provisions survive as their nature requires.
Unauthorized use or disclosure may cause irreparable harm; Mindclade may seek
injunctive relief and any other available remedy, subject to applicable law.

8. No waiver and severability

Failure to enforce a right is not a waiver. An unenforceable provision is
enforced to the maximum extent permitted, and the remaining provisions remain
